WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.901
A secure agent architecture names every source of instruction, data, identity, credential, and action.

2
00:00:06.901 --> 00:00:09.072
Users provide prompts and goals.

3
00:00:09.072 --> 00:00:13.957
System and developer instructions define trusted application behavior.

4
00:00:13.957 --> 00:00:28.302
Retrieved documents, uploaded files, conversation history, memory, tool outputs, model responses, logs, external APIs, identity providers, databases, queues, and background jobs all participate in the workflow.

5
00:00:28.302 --> 00:00:34.505
Each component has a different authority level, and secure design keeps those levels visible.

6
00:00:34.505 --> 00:00:43.577
Trust boundaries are crossed whenever data, instructions, credentials, or control moves between components with different authority.

7
00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:46.989
A user prompt is not the same as a system instruction.

8
00:00:46.989 --> 00:00:50.013
A retrieved document is not the same as policy.

9
00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:53.192
A memory entry is not necessarily current truth.

10
00:00:53.192 --> 00:00:55.983
A model response is not an approved action.

11
00:00:55.983 --> 00:01:02.962
A tool output may be useful evidence, but it may still be incomplete, stale, or scoped to a specific account.

12
00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:08.312
These distinctions shape what the agent is allowed to trust and what it may do next.

13
00:01:08.312 --> 00:01:12.267
Untrusted text should be treated as data rather than policy.

14
00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:27.697
A document, email, ticket, webpage, or uploaded file can contain language that looks like instructions to the model, but it should not be allowed to redefine permissions, grant tool access, disable logging, or override approval gates.

15
00:01:27.697 --> 00:01:32.039
Source labeling and provenance make this distinction practical.

16
00:01:32.039 --> 00:01:39.328
They help the application and the user understand where content came from and how much authority it should have.

17
00:01:39.328 --> 00:01:43.515
Agent architecture should also make responsibility explicit.

18
00:01:43.515 --> 00:01:52.432
Who owns the agent, the tool credentials, the retrieval sources, the memory store, the approval policy, and the incident response path?

19
00:01:52.432 --> 00:01:55.688
Which service is responsible for authorization?

20
00:01:55.688 --> 00:01:58.247
Which component records audit events?

21
00:01:58.247 --> 00:02:00.651
Which process reviews tool changes?

22
00:02:00.651 --> 00:02:09.103
When responsibility is explicit, agent security becomes a maintainable engineering system instead of a set of hopeful prompts.
